take the same arguments, and passing one engine's argument to the other is a
mistake that is easy to make and expensive to notice — a standardized
coefficient table printed under an "original units" heading looks perfectly
plausible. Every extra argument is therefore matched by name against the
selected fitter's own formals, and anything that does not belong is an error
rather than a silently ignored \code{...} entry.
}
\section{Automatic centring and scaling}{
\code{standardize = TRUE} automates the pattern in \code{inst/dev/rpbnb_frank_open.R} and
\code{inst/dev/tmb_rpbnb_frank_open.R}: continuous predictors are centred and scaled
(mean 0, SD 1) before fitting, which keeps a bounded random-coefficient
carrier from acting as a disguised random intercept (see those scripts'
headers) and fixes the design matrix's conditioning when regressors span
very different ranges. Continuous predictors are identified automatically —
numeric, non-factor columns used by either formula with more than two
distinct values, so 0/1 (or any two-level numeric) indicators are left
alone — or supplied explicitly via \code{continuous_vars}. Variables that appear
only inside an \code{offset()} are never standardized.

The fitted design itself (the stored \code{X1}/\code{X2}, \code{mu1}/\code{mu2}, simulation
draws) stays on the standardized scale, exactly as in the two scripts above,
so \code{predict()}, marginal effects, and boundary/LR tests keep working
unchanged. Only the coefficient table \code{print()} and \code{summary()} display is
affected: it is back-transformed to the covariates' original units by the
exact affine chain rule (no refit, no numerical differentiation) and is the
\emph{only} coefficient table shown — there is no separate standardized-scale
table to reconcile. \code{coef()}/\code{vcov()} still return the standardized-scale
values that match the stored design; call \code{.rpbnb_orig_units()}
(internal, mirrors what \code{print()} displays) if the numeric original-units
vector is needed directly. The scaling actually used is stored on the fit
as \verb{$scaling} (a named list of \code{c(center=, scale=)}) and \verb{$continuous_vars}.
}

\section{Boundary LR tests}{
\code{boundary_tests} runs a likelihood-ratio test on the fit as soon as it
converges (against the same data the fit itself used -- the standardized
copy, when \code{standardize = TRUE}) and attaches the result as
\verb{$boundary_tests}. It is a switch over three independent groups, so the
cost is paid only for the parameters actually in question:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{boundary_tests} \tab tests \tab restricted refits \cr
   \code{FALSE} / \code{"none"} \tab nothing \tab 0 \cr
   \code{TRUE} \tab \code{c("sd", "dispersion")} \tab one per scale + one per free \code{m} \cr
   \code{"sd"} \tab random-coefficient scales \tab one per scale \cr
   \code{"dispersion"} \tab overdispersions \code{m1}, \code{m2} \tab one per free \code{m} \cr
   \code{"dependence"} \tab the association parameter \tab 1 \cr
   \code{"all"} \tab all three groups \tab all of the above \cr
}


The random-coefficient SDs and the NB2 dispersions (\code{m1}, \code{m2}) have a null
that sits on the boundary of the parameter space, so an ordinary Wald
\code{z}/\code{p} does not test it; the boundary test refits each restricted model
instead and \code{summary()}/\code{print()} show that test's \code{LR}/\code{df}/\code{p} for those
rows in the natural-scale block, in place of the \code{NA} they would otherwise
carry.

The \strong{dependence} test is the odd one out and is therefore opt-in even
under \code{boundary_tests = TRUE}. Its null is "no association", i.e. the
independence model, which for Famoye (\code{lam}), Frank (\code{theta}), and the
Gaussian copula (\code{rho}) sits in the \emph{interior} of the parameter space --
the Wald \code{z} those rows already show is valid, and the LR statistic is an
ordinary chi-square(1), not the 50:50 boundary mixture. Only Clayton /
Kimeldorf (\code{theta > 0}) has a genuine boundary null there. Requesting it
replaces the dependence row's Wald \code{z}/\code{p} with the LR test on both
engines, since the two answer the same question.

Both engines test the same parameters via \code{\link[=rpbnb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_boundary_tests()}}
(\code{engine = "classic"}) or \code{\link[=rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests()}}
(\code{engine = "tmb"}). They differ only in how each restricted fit is
constructed while preserving common random numbers: for a scale, the
classic engine zeroes that coefficient's draw column while the TMB engine
pins its \code{log_sd} and maps it out of the free parameters; for the
dependence parameter, the TMB engine refits with its own
\code{dependence = "independence"} family while the classic engine (which has no
such fitter) pins the working-scale dependence parameter at its family's
independence value.

A \code{\link[=message]{message()}} reports how many restricted refits are about to run before
they start, unless \code{control$print_level} is \code{0}; suppress it with
\code{\link[=suppressMessages]{suppressMessages()}} if needed.

Under \code{engine = "tmb"} with \code{method = "laplace"}, a restricted refit that
fails to converge is automatically retried with both sides of that one LR
estimated by \code{method = "sml"} rather than reported \code{NA} -- some
restrictions leave Laplace no valid optimum at all (see
\code{\link[=rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests()}}'s \code{sml_fallback} argument, which is where to
turn this off).

\code{disable_parallel_gaussian} (\code{engine = "tmb"} only, passed via \code{...}) is
forwarded to every restricted refit, so a Gaussian-copula fit that opted
out of multithreading gets single-threaded boundary refits too -- see
\code{\link[=rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests()}}'s own \code{disable_parallel_gaussian} argument
for why this needs forwarding at all (the fit object does not record
whether the opt-out was used). The deprecated pre-0.4.6
\code{force_parallel_gaussian} is likewise accepted and mapped (see
\code{\link[=fit_rpbnb_tmb]{fit_rpbnb_tmb()}}).

Each restricted refit costs roughly as much as the original fit (more for
a \code{\link[=copula]{copula()}} dependence than for \code{"famoye"}; see \code{\link[=rpbnb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_boundary_tests()}}'s
timing note), so this defaults to \code{FALSE}. \code{boundary_draws} (\code{engine = "tmb"} only) sets a \code{draws} for those refits other than the main fit's --
e.g. more draws for a more precise boundary test without re-fitting the
whole model at that \code{draws}. It has no classic-engine counterpart:
\code{\link[=rpbnb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_boundary_tests()}} reuses the full fit's exact stored draw matrix
(zeroing a column) rather than regenerating draws from a count, so passing
\code{boundary_draws} under \code{engine = "classic"} is an error. For finer control
still -- testing only \code{"sd"} or only \code{"dispersion"} (both engines take a
\code{which} argument), or reusing one boundary-test run across several
summaries -- call \code{\link[=rpbnb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_boundary_tests()}}/\code{\link[=rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests]{rpbnb_tmb_boundary_tests()}}
directly on the fit and assign its result to \code{fit$boundary_tests} (with
\code{standardize = TRUE}, reconstruct the fitting-scale data first:
\code{rpbnb:::.apply_scaling(data, fit$scaling)}).
}

\section{Which arguments go with which engine}{
\tabular{lll}{
   Argument \tab \code{engine = "classic"} \tab \code{engine = "tmb"} \cr
   \code{draw_type}, \code{.fixed}, \code{.opt_draws} \tab yes \tab error \cr
   \code{inference}, \code{keep} \tab error \tab yes \cr
   \code{method}, \code{disable_parallel_gaussian}, \code{force_parallel_gaussian} \tab ignored with a warning \tab yes \cr
   \code{offset()} in a formula \tab yes \tab error \cr
   \code{dependence = "independence"} \tab error \tab yes \cr
   \code{boundary_draws} (non-\code{NULL}) \tab error \tab yes \cr
   \code{control} class \tab \code{rpbnb_control} \tab \code{rpbnb_control} (same object) \cr
   optimizer \tab \code{maxLik::maxLik(method = "BFGS")} \tab \code{stats::nlminb} + restarts \cr
}


Both engines freeze the Famoye admissible lambda interval at the starting
values (so the analytic gradient is exactly the derivative of the optimized
objective) and validate the fitted lambda against the interval admissible at
the optimum afterwards — check \code{lambda_admissible} on the fit. The
fixed-parameter \code{\link[=fit_bnb]{fit_bnb()}} takes the opposite trade-off (moving bounds,
admissible by construction); see the decision note in \code{R/bnb_likelihood.R}.
}

